# LeadFlow — Cursor Rules

## What this project is
A lead generation platform. Leads are ingested from external sources, validated,
scored, and surfaced to buyers. The pipeline is the product; the vertical
(mortgage, insurance, etc.) is configurable.

## Naming rules — enforce always
- Never use country names in type names, component names, file names, or copy.
  Wrong: CanadaLeadRow, canada-payload.ts, "Canadian mortgage leads"
  Right: LeadRow, mortgage-payload.ts, "mortgage leads"
  Exception: internal data pipeline tooling (e.g. python/) may use jurisdiction and city names when they accurately describe the data source or geo filter being processed.
- Vertical-specific logic lives in lib/processing/verticals/
- Types are market-agnostic. Market/country is a data field, not a type name.

## Domain model
source → raw_record → (processing) → lead → handoff

- raw_records: append-only. Never mutate payload. Processing state lives here.
- leads: promoted records only. A lead exists because it passed qualification.
- handoff: when a lead is sold/delivered to a buyer.
- Source attribution on a raw_record is set at ingest and is immutable.
- Qualification scores are always computed server-side, never client-side.

## Silent failure rules — the most important rules in this file
The processing pipeline has three failure modes. All three must produce
observable output. Never write code that silently swallows these:

1. Soft skips (invalid_payload, no_phone, below_threshold, already_sold):
   - Must store a skip_reason on the raw_record row. Not just skipped++.
   - Must be individually retrievable. "N rows skipped" is not enough.

2. Hard failures (thrown errors in processOneRow):
   - Must write processing_error to the raw_record row. (Already done.)
   - Must also emit a structured log entry with rawRecordId and error message.
   - finalizeFailure double-failures must not be swallowed. Re-throw or log.

3. Batch-level failures (RPC errors, claim failures):
   - Must emit a structured log entry before returning 500.
   - The log must include: timestamp, batch size attempted, error message.

Structured logging means: console.error(JSON.stringify({...})) at minimum today.
When Sentry is added, these become Sentry.captureException() calls with context.
Never use console.log for errors. Only console.error with a JSON payload.

## API routes
- Every route in app/api/ must be wrapped in withMonitoring().
- withMonitoring() is in lib/api.ts. Use it. Do not inline its logic.
- Every route must declare a flow tag:
  lead-capture | processing | qualification | handoff | admin | preview
- A catch block that only calls console.log, or is empty, is a build error.

## Supabase
- supabase db push --linked --include-all. Never push individual migrations.
- No seed data in migration files. Seed data in supabase/seed.sql.
- Always set Cache-Control: no-store on Supabase clients.
- Generated types in lib/database.types.ts. Run db:gen-types after every migration.
- RLS: no anon access. Service role on the server only.

## Validation
- Zod for all external input. No exceptions.
- Zod schemas live in lib/validation/. One file per vertical/context.
- A Zod parse failure is not a success. Do not call finalizeSuccess on parse failure.
  (This is a known bug in the current codebase. Fix it when you touch that path.)

## Testing
- Every new lib/ function gets a unit test in the same commit.
- Every new API route gets an integration test in the same commit.
- npm test must pass before every commit.
- Tests are reported pass/fail only. No console noise in test output.

## TypeScript
- No `any` without a comment explaining why it cannot be avoided.
- Strict mode is on. Do not work around it.

## Documentation
- New migration → update schema section of docs/STATE.md.
- New env var → add to docs/env-vars.md and .env.example.
- Non-obvious decision → create ADR in docs/adr/.
- Do not update docs that did not change.

## AI assistant behavior
- Read before editing. Explore relevant files first.
- Edit minimally. Change only what the task requires.
- Do not refactor code you were not asked to refactor.
- Do not rename things without being asked.
- Exception: if you encounter a silent catch block or a finalizeSuccess-on-parse-failure
  while working on adjacent code, flag it as a finding. Do not fix it silently.
- When adding an API route: add withMonitoring() automatically.
- When adding a lib/ function: add its test automatically.
- Before any DB command: state what it will do and wait for confirmation.
- Never push to prod without explicit instruction.
- Never use country names in generated code, types, or copy.
- **SR&ED workflow:** During substantive work, maintain `docs/sred/wip-<branch-slug>.md`
  per "SR&ED contemporaneous notes & commit workflow" below. When the user asks for a
  commit message or says they are about to commit, compile from that file + diff per
  that section.

# SR&ED contemporaneous notes & commit workflow (assistant must follow)

Evidence lives in **files**, not chat. Chat is not reliable across sessions.

## WIP note file (one per git branch)

- **Path:** `docs/sred/wip-<branch-slug>.md`
- **`<branch-slug>`:** output of `git branch --show-current` with each `/` replaced by
  `-` (e.g. `feature/capture` → `docs/sred/wip-feature-capture.md`).
- If the file does not exist, **create it** the first time a note below applies.
- **Do not** put secrets, credentials, or personal data in this file; reference test
  fixtures or redacted summaries instead.

## When to append notes (during the same session as the work)

While implementing or investigating, **append a new bullet or short subsection** under
the top-level `## WIP entries` section (create that section if missing) when **any**
of these occur:

- Technological **uncertainty** (could not decide from docs/patterns alone).
- A **hypothesis** or planned approach before coding.
- An approach **tried** and **rejected** or **insufficient** (include why, specifically).
- A **measurement** or test outcome (or explicitly that measurement was not possible yet).
- A **non-obvious trade-off** that affects architecture, data integrity, concurrency, or
  correctness.

**Do not** use this file for routine work that matches "Auto-Disqualify Conditions" in
the SR&ED rule below unless you add a single line: `- Routine: <one phrase> (no SRED thread).`

**UI / styling-only** changes: no SR&ED narrative; at most one routine line as above.

**Honesty:** Never invent failures or experiments that did not happen. If something was
not recorded at the time, write `Not recorded in WIP` rather than backfilling fiction.

Use **first person**, **specific** tools/APIs/constraints, and reuse an existing
**Project Tag** (see SR&ED section) when continuing the same thread of uncertainty.

## When the user asks for a commit message or indicates they are about to commit

1. Read **`docs/sred/wip-<branch-slug>.md`** for the current branch (if present).
2. Read **`git diff`** (staged if available, else working tree) relevant to the commit.
3. Write a **conventional commit subject** plus body that summarizes **user-visible /
   maintainable** changes (not the SR&ED story alone). **This part is mandatory** — the
   commit message must always describe what changed for humans/reviewers, not only SR&ED.
4. Apply the **Mandatory Pre-Check** in the SR&ED section below to the **substantive**
   technical work in this commit (pipeline, data, concurrency, algorithms — **not** UI-only).
5. In the **same** commit message, after the conventional body, append **exactly one** SR&ED outcome:
   - If the full technical log is justified per those rules: include the full
     **SRED TECHNICAL LOG** (sections 0–8), grounded in **WIP entries + diff**. If WIP
     lacks a required failure/insufficiency for a full log, use
     `SRED: Not applicable (routine work)` instead — do not invent.
   - Else: append a single line:
     `SRED: Not applicable (routine work)`
6. **Archive consumed WIP bullets:** Move entries that informed this commit into a
   `## Archived (YYYY-MM-DD)` section in the **same** file (or delete only if the user
   asked for a minimal file — default is **archive in place**). Leave `## WIP entries`
   ready for the next commit.

## Optional human trigger

For a one-shot instruction, the user can run the prompt in
`.cursor/prompts/commit-from-sred-wip.md`.

# SR&ED Documentation Rule (Audit-Grade, Enforced)

## Mandatory Pre-Check (MUST RUN FIRST)

Before writing anything, determine:

- Was there a technological uncertainty that could NOT be solved using standard documentation or known patterns?
- Was at least one approach attempted, tested, or proven insufficient?
- Did this work generate new technical knowledge (not just implementation)?

IF ALL are TRUE:
→ Write full SRED TECHNICAL LOG

ELSE:
→ Write exactly:
SRED: Not applicable (routine work)
→ DO NOT write any additional SR&ED content

---

## SRED TECHNICAL LOG:

0. **Project Tag**
- Assign a persistent ID (e.g., AUTH-PERF-01, SCRAPER-ANTI-BOT-02)
- Reuse this tag across ALL commits related to the same uncertainty

1. **Technological Uncertainty**
- What specific capability was unknown or unproven?
- Why could this NOT be solved using standard practice, documentation, or known solutions?
- What existing approaches/tools were insufficient or failed?

2. **Baseline State**
- What was the system doing BEFORE this work?
- Include measurable constraints (latency, error rate, scale limits, cost, reliability)

3. **Hypothesis**
- What did I believe would resolve the uncertainty?
- Why did I expect this approach to work?

4. **Experimentation**
- What EXACT changes were implemented? (algorithms, architecture, queries, infra)
- What variants or alternatives were tested?
- What FAILED and WHY? (REQUIRED if any failure occurred)

5. **Results & Evidence**
- What happened after implementation?
- Include measurable results where possible (latency, throughput, error rate, cost)
- Directly compare against baseline

## Comparison Requirement:
- Results MUST explicitly compare against Baseline State
- If no comparison is made → the log is invalid

6. **Technological Advancement**
- What NEW capability or knowledge was gained?
- How does this reduce uncertainty going forward?
- Is this reusable beyond this specific task?
- Field of Science/Tech: Explicitly state the sub-field (e.g., Computer Science - Distributed Systems or Information Technology - Data Privacy).

7. **Continuity (REQUIRED if applicable)**
- Reference prior related commits or experiments
- Explain how this builds on previous hypotheses or failures

8. **Effort Indicator (Lightweight)**
- Relative effort: Low / Medium / High
- Brief justification (e.g., multiple failed approaches, iterative experimentation, single test)

---

## Critical Rules:

- PRIORITIZE FAILURE: If nothing failed, it is likely NOT SR&ED eligible
- FAILURE REQUIREMENT: If SRED TECHNICAL LOG is written, it MUST include:
  - At least one clearly stated failed or insufficient approach
  - A specific technical reason why it failed
  - If no failure exists → DO NOT write SR&ED log

- NO GENERIC STATEMENTS: Avoid vague phrases like "improved performance"
- NO UI WORK: Ignore styling, layout, or marketing content
- FIRST PERSON ONLY: Use "I investigated", "I tested"
- BE SPECIFIC: Name tools, libraries, APIs, constraints
- MINIMUM DETAIL: Each section must contain concrete technical detail (no filler)

---

## Measurement Rule:
If no measurable metric is included in "Results & Evidence",
explicitly state why measurement was not possible.

---

## Auto-Disqualify Conditions:
If the work is ANY of the following, mark "Not applicable":
- CRUD wiring or basic API usage
- straightforward implementation from documentation
- UI/styling changes
- config-only changes with no uncertainty
- known pattern implementation without experimentation

---

## Anti-Fluff Enforcement:

Do NOT exaggerate or reframe routine work as experimental.

Disallowed phrases unless supported by evidence:
- "novel", "advanced", "innovative", "AI-powered", "optimized"

Allowed ONLY if supported by:
- a clearly stated technical limitation
- at least one failed or insufficient existing approach
- measurable constraints or trade-offs

---

## Reframing Rule (Evidence-Based):

When describing work, frame it in terms of:

❌ WHAT was built  
→ Avoid: "Built X system"

✅ WHAT COULD NOT BE DONE + WHY  
→ "I could not achieve [specific capability] using [standard approach] due to [technical limitation]"

Then:

→ "I hypothesized [approach] would resolve this by [mechanism]"
→ "This failed/succeeded because [technical reason]"